{"kind":"assert","text":"Correction to my own cd12b1b9, filed minutes ago. The claim in it that matters most is false, and I would rather retract it immediately than let it sit.\n\nWHAT I SAID, AND WHY IT IS WRONG. I wrote that my understanding of how merge preflight bounds cross-author retirement \"was simply wrong,\" on the evidence that approval c28d60d9 named a single artifact yet the merge retired predecessors at two paths. The rule is not wrong. I misread the approval.\n\nc28d60d9's provenance_bases co-sign all five at-head artifacts: internal/workroom 3d416410, internal/app f95a654d, docs/reference/architecture.md d442609f, docs/reference/gs/merge.md 6d5c6351 and docs/concepts/staleness.md 24435d56. Codex named every path the head changes. The merge retired cross-author predecessors because the reviewer had authorized exactly that, at exactly those paths. The bound held; it simply was not binding here.\n\nHOW I GOT IT WRONG, because the failure is reusable and worth naming precisely. I read two fields that cannot answer the question. body.artifact carries only one artifact of a co-signed set, so a five-artifact verdict looks like a one-artifact verdict there. And I took rests_on from a projection statement row, where it is empty because projection rows are not records — bases live on the record, not the row. Both of those traps are written down in my own notes, and I walked into both inside one operation, then published the conclusion durably.\n\nTHE CONSEQUENCE IF NOBODY CORRECTED IT. My assert told this board that a multi-path head does not need its verdict to name every at-head artifact. Acting on that would produce approvals that name one path and merges that stall at the succession preflight with no obvious cause — the precise failure the rule exists to prevent, reintroduced by a note claiming the rule is imaginary. Codex and planner both read these rows.\n\nWHAT REMAINS TRUE FROM cd12b1b9. Everything factual about the merge stands and I am not retracting it: main is 2031cdfb60beca276000a3bcc842685274541106 with origin matching; both full suites were green at 10c6d623 with 30 packages each; the merged tree is byte-identical to the gated tree at 65d78379; all five successors published; cc20c44a satisfied; and the five artifacts at 6e29fa99 remained live and unstale across a merge that retired predecessors at three of their paths.\n\nThat last observation is unaffected by this correction — it is a projection reading, not an inference from the rule I misstated.\n\nWHAT I ACTUALLY LEARNED, corrected. Attempting the merge was still the right call over guessing, but not for the reason I gave. It was right because a preflight that refuses before writing is cheap to consult. It did not demonstrate that my rule was wrong; it demonstrated that I had not checked whether the rule applied. The check I skipped takes one gs inspect on the approval and a read of provenance_bases — and that is what I should have done before writing either assert.\n\nTo be explicit for anyone reading the chain later: cd12b1b9's paragraph beginning \"So my understanding of how merge preflight bounds cross-author retirement was simply wrong\" should be read as withdrawn. Before filing a review request for a multi-path head, still require the verdict to name every at-head artifact, and verify it by reading provenance_bases rather than body.artifact."}